      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sumneytown was named after early settler Issac Sumney, who opened the Red Lion inn in 1762.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumneytown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1848 the Sumneytown and Spring House Turnpike opened, linking the village to Marlborough and drawing Philadelphia's wealthy families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        19,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumneytown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic Kings Highway ran through Sumneytown, and the stretch north to the Lehigh County line is now Geryville Pike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumneytown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Unami Creek forms Sumneytown's southeastern boundary, flowing southwest into the Perkiomen Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        27,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumneytown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Route 63, which passes through Sumneytown, began as the Sumneytown and Spring House Turnpike and continues to serve the area today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumneytown%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Sumneytown experiences a hot-summer humid continental climate, with July averages around 75°F and January lows near 30°F.",
